\chapter{Raw stdlib bridges and snapshot conventions}
\label{ch:98}
\index{Raw stdlib bridges and snapshot conventions}
\index{FFI!raw bridges}

Raw bridges expose exact, narrow C signatures to source wrapper modules. The build helper chooses bridge groups and compiles the bridge separately from the shared kernel implementation. Status values or out-of-band bounded snapshots can carry more information through the scalar ABI. This approach can avoid exposing native handles directly. Ownership inside the bridge remains the responsibility of its C implementation.

\section{Test a snapshot lifecycle}
Choose a text or byte operation. Test an ordinary success, zero-length data, and failure. Verify the documented lifetime by issuing a later operation and checking what happens to the earlier result. Exercise allocation failure where the bridge supplies fault injection, and check that temporary resources are released. Look for NUL-free restrictions on text even where the paired byte API allows zeros. A bridge's trust comes from its concrete implementation and evidence; its source wrapper signature alone does not supply reviewed foreign admission.

\section{Count live copies in the INI bridge}
The allocation fixture replaces the bridge allocator, keeps the INI
kernel separately compiled, and counts outstanding bridge allocations.
Read its first loop as two different experiments. With zero allocations
permitted, looking up the key in a valid assignment returns empty text
and a memory status. With one allocation permitted, the lookup can
start constructing its answer but must still return empty text and the
same memory status. The count of live copies is zero at the end of
each attempt. Checking only the string would miss both a leak after
partial construction and a mistaken report of success.

Next allow allocation and ask for a key whose assigned value is empty.
The returned bytes look identical, but the status is now success and
one allocation is retained. Follow it with a document containing a good
assignment followed by an invalid line. Finding the desired key early
does not make the whole document valid: the expected status is parse
failure and the retained count does not increase. Sample the bridge's
status immediately alongside its payload; the next operation supplies a
new status.

Finally the fixture uses a deliberately small pool. Successful lookups
consume space until another snapshot will not fit. Repeating a lookup
at that point must report the limit again without incrementing the live
count. The exit check expects cleanup to return the count to zero.
These observations distinguish per-call temporary copies from retained
snapshots without exposing either allocation address to source code.

For two successful reads, saving the first text is legitimate for
this bridge, but saving only a reference to the current status is not
a record of the first read. Save the status value with its text.

